"""Original MechIntel revision lessons. Questions are authored, not official PYQs."""
def chapter(slug, title, concept, formula, example, solution, mistakes, practice):
    return dict(slug=slug, title=title, concept=concept, formula=formula, example=example,
                solution=solution, mistakes=mistakes, practice=practice)

BOOKS = [
dict(slug='engineering-mathematics', title='Engineering Mathematics', subtitle='Matrices, calculus, differential equations and probability',
 gaps='Vector calculus, complex variables, Fourier series, partial differential equations, numerical methods and advanced statistics need additional study.', chapters=[
chapter('linear-algebra', 'Linear algebra: equations and eigenvalues',
 'A system Ax = b connects unknown quantities through linear constraints. Row operations preserve its solutions. For a square coefficient matrix, a nonzero determinant guarantees a unique solution; a zero determinant requires checking consistency rather than automatically declaring that there is no solution. Compare the rank of A with the rank of the augmented matrix [A|b]. Equal ranks give a consistent system; if this rank is below the number of unknowns, free variables remain. An eigenvector keeps its direction when multiplied by A, although its magnitude or sign can change. This makes eigenvalues useful in vibration and stability analysis. Find eigenvalues from the characteristic polynomial before solving for eigenvectors. For a two-by-two matrix, the sum of eigenvalues equals its trace and their product equals its determinant; use both as arithmetic checks.',
 'For A = [[a,b],[c,d]]: det(A) = ad − bc; det(A − λI) = 0. Consistency: rank(A) = rank([A|b]).',
 'Solve 2x + y = 7 and x − y = 2. Then find the eigenvalues of A = [[2,1],[1,2]].',
 'Add the two equations: 3x = 9, so x = 3 and y = 1. For the second matrix, (2 − λ)² − 1 = 0, giving λ = 1 and 3. Trace = 4 and determinant = 3 agree with the sum and product.',
 'Do not confuse the coefficient matrix with the eigenvalue example matrix. A singular matrix can describe either inconsistent equations or infinitely many solutions.',
 [('Find the determinant of [[4,2],[1,3]].','10. Compute 4×3 − 2×1.'),('Are x+y=2 and 2x+2y=5 consistent?','No. Doubling the first equation gives 2x+2y=4, contradicting the second.')]),
chapter('calculus', 'Calculus: stationary points and definite integrals',
 'A derivative measures local change. A stationary point is a candidate optimum, not a guarantee: test the second derivative or compare nearby values. For a constrained interval, also evaluate the endpoints. A definite integral accumulates a signed quantity; negative values of the integrand subtract from the result. In engineering, identify the physical quantity before integrating: integrating a velocity gives displacement, whereas integrating speed gives travelled distance. For a function of several variables, a partial derivative holds the other independent variables fixed. When variables are linked, use the total derivative and chain rule. Always carry units through the calculation. An integral of a force with respect to distance has energy units, while an integral of a force with respect to time has momentum units.',
 'Stationary point: f′(x)=0. If f″(x)>0: local minimum; if f″(x)<0: local maximum. ∫aᵇ f(x) dx = F(b) − F(a).',
 'Find the minimum of f(x)=x²−6x+13 on the real line and evaluate ∫₀² (3x²+1) dx.',
 'f′=2x−6=0 gives x=3. Since f″=2>0 and the parabola opens upward, the global minimum is f(3)=4. The antiderivative of 3x²+1 is x³+x. Its change from 0 to 2 is 8+2=10.',
 'When the second derivative is zero the test is inconclusive. Do not omit the lower integration limit or confuse signed displacement with distance.',
 [('Find the minimum of x²−4x+8.','4 at x=2; complete the square to obtain (x−2)²+4.'),('Evaluate ∫₀¹ 2x dx.','1; evaluate x² between 0 and 1.')]),
chapter('differential-equations', 'Differential equations: first-order response',
 'A first-order differential equation describes how a quantity changes in response to its present state and a forcing input. Separate variables only when the expression can genuinely be written as one function of y times one function of x. For a linear equation y′+P(x)y=Q(x), an integrating factor turns the left side into a product derivative. Apply the initial condition after obtaining the general solution. In a stable constant-coefficient response, the transient decays and the solution approaches its steady value. The time constant tells you the time scale of that decay: after one time constant, a zero-input exponential has fallen to 1/e of its initial value. Check the solution by substitution into the original equation and initial condition, not only by comparing a remembered formula.',
 'Integrating factor = exp(∫P dx). For dy/dt + ay = b with a>0: y(t)=b/a + [y(0)−b/a]exp(−at); time constant τ=1/a.',
 'Solve dy/dt + 2y = 6 for y(0)=1, with time in seconds.',
 'The steady value is 6/2=3. The transient coefficient is 1−3=−2, so y(t)=3−2exp(−2t). At t=0 this gives 1. Its derivative is 4exp(−2t); adding 2y gives 6. The time constant is 0.5 s.',
 'The exponent must be dimensionless. A positive growth exponent is not a stable decay. Keep the homogeneous and particular solutions distinct.',
 [('For dy/dt+4y=0 and y(0)=8, find y at t=ln(2)/4 s.','4. The exponential factor is exp(−ln 2)=1/2.'),('What is the steady value for dy/dt+5y=20?','4, obtained by setting the time derivative to zero.')]),
chapter('probability', 'Probability: conditional events and expectation',
 'Conditional probability changes the sample space to outcomes for which the conditioning event occurred. Independence is stronger than simply observing that two events differ; it requires their joint probability to equal the product of their probabilities. Mutually exclusive events with nonzero probabilities cannot be independent. Expected value is a probability-weighted average, not necessarily a value the random variable can actually take. For independent repeated trials with two outcomes and a fixed success probability, use the binomial model. Check that the probability stays constant and that one trial does not affect another. Sampling without replacement from a small finite population usually violates that independence assumption. Before calculating, decide whether the problem concerns exactly k successes, at least k, or no successes.',
 'P(A|B)=P(A∩B)/P(B), P(B)>0. E[X]=Σx p(x). Binomial: P(X=k)=C(n,k)pᵏ(1−p)ⁿ⁻ᵏ; E[X]=np.',
 'Three independent components each have a defect probability of 0.1. Find the probability of exactly one defect.',
 'There are C(3,1)=3 possible defective positions. Each arrangement has probability 0.1×0.9². Thus P(X=1)=3×0.1×0.81=0.243. The expected number of defects is 3×0.1=0.3, which is not the probability of one defect.',
 'Do not use the defect count as a probability. For “at least one”, use 1 minus the probability of zero defects.',
 [('Two independent components each fail with probability 0.2. Find the probability that at least one fails.','0.36. Use 1−0.8².'),('If P(A∩B)=0.12 and P(B)=0.4, find P(A|B).','0.3, from 0.12/0.4.')])]),
dict(slug='applied-mechanics-design', title='Applied Mechanics & Design', subtitle='Equilibrium, axial loading, torsion and vibration',
 gaps='Friction, trusses, rigid-body dynamics, beam shear and bending, stress transformation, columns, mechanisms, gears, fatigue, machine elements, forced vibration and control systems need additional study.', chapters=[
chapter('equilibrium', 'Engineering mechanics: free-body diagrams',
 'Start statics by isolating a body and showing every external force and couple. Replace supports with reactions appropriate to the idealization. A pin in a plane can supply two reaction components but no reaction moment; a smooth roller supplies a reaction normal to its surface. Choose a moment center that eliminates unknown forces and reduces algebra. A couple is a free moment and does not need a lever arm. For distributed loads, replace the load by its resultant at the centroid of the distribution before solving reactions. After calculating, check vertical and horizontal force balance and take moments about a second point. A negative reaction indicates a direction opposite to the initial assumption; whether the support can provide it is a separate physical question.',
 'Planar rigid-body equilibrium: ΣFx=0, ΣFy=0, ΣMz=0. Uniform distributed load: resultant W=wL at the midpoint of the loaded length.',
 'A simply supported 4 m beam carries a downward 10 kN point load 1 m from its left support. Find both vertical reactions.',
 'Take moments about the left support: RB×4−10×1=0, hence RB=2.5 kN upward. Vertical balance gives RA=10−2.5=7.5 kN upward. Checking moments about the right: 7.5×4−10×3=0.',
 'A pin support is not a fixed support. Use the distance perpendicular to a force line when calculating its moment.',
 [('A 6 m simply supported beam carries a central 12 kN load. Find the reactions.','6 kN upward at each support by symmetry and force balance.'),('A 3 m beam carries a uniform 2 kN/m load over its full length. What is the equivalent load and location?','6 kN downward, acting 1.5 m from either end.')]),
chapter('axial-stress-strain', 'Mechanics of materials: axial stress and extension',
 'Normal stress is an internal force per unit area. The familiar uniform axial formula describes a straight prismatic bar sufficiently far from load application and geometric discontinuities. Engineering strain is extension divided by original length. Within linear elasticity, stress and strain are proportional through Young’s modulus. If a bar has multiple segments, calculate each segment’s extension using its own length, area and modulus, then sum compatible deformations. Thermal expansion produces strain even without mechanical stress; a freely expanding bar can change length without a thermal force. Restraint can create stress, so the support conditions matter. State whether the quoted modulus uses Pa, GPa or N/mm², and use a consistent length and force system throughout.',
 'σ=P/A; ε=δ/L; σ=Eε; δ=PL/(AE). Free thermal extension: δth=αLΔT.',
 'A 1000 mm long bar has area 200 mm² and E=200000 N/mm². Find stress and extension under a 20 kN tensile force.',
 'Convert 20 kN to 20000 N. Stress is 20000/200=100 N/mm²=100 MPa. Extension is (20000×1000)/(200×200000)=0.5 mm. Strain is 0.5/1000=0.0005, consistent with σ/E.',
 'Do not insert E=200 with forces in N and areas in mm²; 200 GPa equals 200000 N/mm². Stress concentration and plastic behavior need separate models.',
 [('A 10 kN force acts on 100 mm². Find nominal stress.','100 MPa, because 10000 N/100 mm²=100 N/mm².'),('A free 2 m bar has α=12×10⁻⁶/K and is heated by 50 K. Find its extension.','1.2 mm. Compute 12×10⁻⁶×2×50 m = 0.0012 m.')]),
chapter('shaft-torsion', 'Torsion of a circular shaft',
 'A circular shaft under elastic torsion develops shear stress that increases with radius. For a solid shaft it is zero at the center and largest at the surface. The polar second moment of area is a geometric quantity with length to the fourth power; it is not the mass moment of inertia. The simple torsion relation assumes a circular prismatic shaft, linear elastic material, small deformation and suitable torque application. Hollow shafts use the difference between outer and inner fourth powers. Calculate torque consistently: N·m must be converted to N·mm if the diameter is in mm. Angular twist from TL/(GJ) is in radians. For stepped shafts, add signed twists and ensure the internal torque in each segment is correct.',
 'Solid circular shaft: J=πd⁴/32; τmax=16T/(πd³); θ=TL/(GJ). Hollow shaft: J=π(D⁴−d⁴)/32.',
 'A solid 40 mm diameter shaft transmits 500 N·m. Find its nominal maximum shear stress.',
 'T=500000 N·mm. Therefore τmax=16×500000/(π×40³)=39.79 N/mm², approximately 39.8 MPa. Doubling diameter at unchanged torque reduces this nominal stress by a factor of eight.',
 'Polar area moment uses d⁴, while maximum stress scales with d³. Keyways and shoulders introduce local effects absent from this formula.',
 [('At fixed torque, what happens to shear stress when diameter doubles?','It becomes one eighth of the original stress because τmax is proportional to 1/d³.'),('Convert 250 N·m into N·mm.','250000 N·mm; one metre equals 1000 millimetres.')]),
chapter('free-vibration', 'Vibrations: natural frequency and damping',
 'An ideal spring-mass system stores energy alternately in spring deformation and mass motion. Its undamped natural frequency depends on stiffness divided by mass. Increasing stiffness raises the frequency; increasing mass lowers it. Damping dissipates energy and reduces free-response amplitude. The damping ratio compares the actual viscous damping coefficient with its critical value. An underdamped system oscillates while its envelope decays; critically damped and overdamped systems do not oscillate in this ideal model. The damped oscillation frequency is below the undamped natural frequency. Natural angular frequency is in radians per second, whereas ordinary frequency is cycles per second. Keep them separate when comparing excitation to a machine speed given in rpm. Forced response and isolation require additional analysis beyond this free-vibration chapter.',
 'ωn=√(k/m); fn=ωn/(2π); cc=2√(km); ζ=c/cc. For 0≤ζ<1: ωd=ωn√(1−ζ²).',
 'A 4 kg mass is attached to a 1600 N/m spring. Find its undamped frequency and critical viscous damping coefficient.',
 'ωn=√(1600/4)=20 rad/s. Hence fn=20/(2π)=3.183 Hz. Critical damping is 2√(1600×4)=160 N·s/m. A damper with c=16 N·s/m would give ζ=0.1.',
 'Do not label 20 rad/s as 20 Hz. Stiffness in N/mm must be converted to N/m when mass is in kg and time in seconds.',
 [('If mass is quadrupled at fixed stiffness, how does natural frequency change?','It halves, since frequency scales with 1/√m.'),('For c=40 N·s/m and cc=200 N·s/m, find ζ and response class.','ζ=0.2, so the free response is underdamped.')])]),
dict(slug='thermal-fluid-sciences', title='Thermal & Fluid Sciences', subtitle='First law, flow energy, thermal resistance and ideal cycles',
 gaps='Pure-substance tables, entropy, exergy, compressible flow, boundary layers, convection correlations, radiation networks, refrigeration, psychrometry and turbomachinery need additional study.', chapters=[
chapter('first-law', 'Thermodynamics: closed-system energy balance',
 'Define the system boundary before applying the first law. In a closed system mass stays inside, but energy can cross the boundary as heat or work. Use the sign convention explicitly: heat into the system is positive and work done by the system is positive here. Internal energy is a state property, while heat and work depend on the path. A cycle returns every state property to its initial value, so its net internal-energy change is zero. For an ideal gas with constant specific heat, internal-energy change depends only on temperature. Boundary work is the integral of pressure with respect to volume for the appropriate quasi-equilibrium path; constant pressure is only one special case. Include kinetic and potential energy when their changes are relevant. A steady-flow device such as a turbine instead requires an open-system balance.',
 'Closed system: ΔU+ΔKE+ΔPE=Q−W. Ideal gas, constant cv: ΔU=mcv(T2−T1). Constant-pressure boundary work: W=p(V2−V1).',
 'A closed stationary gas receives 120 kJ of heat and performs 45 kJ of work. Find its internal-energy change.',
 'Kinetic and potential energy changes are zero. With heat in and work out both positive, ΔU=Q−W=120−45=75 kJ. The gas stores the part of the energy input that did not leave as work.',
 'Heat is not a property stored in a system. Do not use cp for ideal-gas internal-energy change; use cv under the stated constant-specific-heat approximation.',
 [('A closed system rejects 20 kJ of heat and receives 35 kJ of work. Find ΔU when other energy changes vanish.','+15 kJ. Here Q=−20 and W=−35, so Q−W=15.'),('For a complete closed-system cycle, Qnet=80 kJ. Find Wnet.','80 kJ, since the total energy change over a cycle is zero.')]),
chapter('bernoulli-continuity', 'Fluid mechanics: continuity and Bernoulli',
 'Continuity expresses mass conservation. In steady incompressible flow through a single streamtube, area times mean velocity is constant. A smaller area therefore means a larger mean speed. Bernoulli’s equation relates pressure, velocity and elevation along a streamline under its ideal assumptions. For real pipe systems, use an extended mechanical-energy equation that includes pump head, turbine head and irreversible losses. A constant pipe diameter does not imply constant pressure: friction, elevation change and devices can still alter pressure. Specify whether pressure is gauge or absolute; differences can use gauge values consistently, while cavitation and gas calculations need absolute pressure. Before substituting, identify whether a pressure difference is caused by velocity change, elevation change or a loss term.',
 'Steady incompressible continuity: A1V1=A2V2. Ideal Bernoulli: p/(ρg)+V²/(2g)+z=constant. Reynolds number: Re=ρVD/μ.',
 'Water flows through a horizontal ideal contraction from diameter 100 mm at 2 m/s to diameter 50 mm. Find outlet speed and p1−p2. Use ρ=1000 kg/m³.',
 'The area ratio is (100/50)²=4, so V2=8 m/s. With equal elevation and negligible losses, p1−p2=ρ(V2²−V1²)/2=1000(64−4)/2=30000 Pa=30 kPa.',
 'Area scales with the square of diameter. Bernoulli without a loss term is not a general frictional-pipe formula.',
 [('A pipe area halves in steady incompressible flow. How does mean speed change?','It doubles, assuming no branch flow.'),('Find Re for ρ=1000 kg/m³, V=1 m/s, D=0.02 m and μ=0.001 Pa·s.','20000. Compute 1000×1×0.02/0.001.')]),
chapter('thermal-resistance', 'Heat transfer: conduction and thermal resistance',
 'Thermal resistance converts a heat-transfer problem into temperature difference divided by resistance. For one-dimensional steady conduction through a plane wall with constant conductivity and no internal generation, resistance is thickness divided by conductivity times area. At a convecting surface, the resistance is one divided by heat-transfer coefficient times area. Layers in series carry the same heat rate, so their resistances add. Parallel heat-flow paths share a temperature difference and their conductances add. The wall formula is not suitable for a cylinder whose conduction area varies with radius; use the logarithmic cylindrical expression instead. Distinguish total heat rate in watts from heat flux in watts per square metre. Contact resistance and radiation can change the network and should be included when the problem specifies them.',
 'Plane wall: Rcond=L/(kA), Rconv=1/(hA), Q̇=ΔT/Rtotal. Cylinder: Rcond=ln(ro/ri)/(2πkL). Heat flux q″=Q̇/A.',
 'A plane wall is 0.1 m thick, has k=0.5 W/(m·K) and area 2 m². Its face temperatures differ by 40 K. Find steady heat flow.',
 'Rcond=0.1/(0.5×2)=0.1 K/W. Q̇=40/0.1=400 W, flowing from the hotter face to the colder face. The corresponding heat flux is 400/2=200 W/m².',
 'Temperature differences in K and °C have the same numerical value, but radiation requires absolute temperatures. Do not mistake W for W/m².',
 [('Two series resistances are 0.2 and 0.3 K/W with ΔT=100 K. Find heat rate.','200 W. Total resistance is 0.5 K/W.'),('At h=25 W/(m²·K) and A=2 m², find convection resistance.','0.02 K/W, from 1/(25×2).')]),
chapter('ideal-cycles', 'Power cycles: Carnot limit and Otto efficiency',
 'A heat engine receives energy from a hot source, rejects energy to a colder sink and delivers net work. Thermal efficiency compares work output with heat input. A reversible engine between two constant-temperature reservoirs sets an upper bound for engines operating between those reservoirs. Reservoir temperatures must be absolute. The air-standard Otto cycle models two isentropic and two constant-volume processes with an ideal working gas; the constant-specific-heat efficiency formula is a model result rather than a prediction of a real engine’s efficiency. It increases with compression ratio when the ratio of specific heats is fixed. Real engines face combustion, heat-transfer, mechanical and operating constraints. In cycle calculations, first identify process types and then evaluate state relationships before adding heat and work terms.',
 'ηth=Wnet/Qin. Carnot: η=1−Tc/Th. Constant-γ air-standard Otto: η=1−1/r^(γ−1), where r=Vmax/Vmin.',
 'Find the maximum reversible heat-engine efficiency between 600 K and 300 K, and its work output for heat input of 100 kJ.',
 'ηCarnot=1−300/600=0.5, or 50%. Work is ηQin=50 kJ, and heat rejection is 100−50=50 kJ. These values apply to the stated reversible engine, not automatically to an actual engine.',
 'Never insert Celsius reservoir temperatures into the Carnot ratio. Compression ratio is a volume ratio, not a pressure ratio.',
 [('Find Carnot efficiency between 800 K and 400 K.','50%, from 1−400/800.'),('An engine receives 200 kJ and rejects 140 kJ per cycle. Find efficiency.','30%. Net work is 60 kJ, and 60/200=0.30.')])]),
dict(slug='manufacturing-industrial-engineering', title='Manufacturing & Industrial Engineering', subtitle='Casting, tool life, fits and inventory',
 gaps='Materials and phase diagrams, forming, welding, additive manufacturing, metrology instruments, CNC/PLC, scheduling, quality, linear programming, queues and project networks need additional study.', chapters=[
chapter('casting-solidification', 'Casting: solidification time and modulus',
 'A casting freezes as heat leaves through the mould interface. A common comparative model relates solidification time to the volume-to-cooling-area ratio, called the casting modulus. Volume determines how much material must cool, while the exposed area helps transfer heat. For geometrically similar shapes, volume scales with the cube of length and area with the square, so the modulus scales directly with length. Compare times only when the model constant and exponent are applicable to both castings: the alloy, mould and thermal conditions matter. A riser must feed shrinkage during solidification, so its freezing behavior and connection to the casting matter as well as its size. A modulus calculation alone cannot establish a complete gating or feeding design. Identify which surfaces actually cool before computing the relevant area.',
 'Chvorinov model: ts=C(V/A)^n. For equal C and n: ts2/ts1=[(V2/A2)/(V1/A1)]^n. Often n≈2 is assumed in an exercise.',
 'For geometrically similar castings in the same mould conditions, linear size doubles. Find the solidification-time ratio when n=2.',
 'Volume increases eightfold and cooling area fourfold, so V/A doubles. With unchanged C and n=2, time increases by 2²=4. The result is a ratio and does not require knowing C.',
 'Do not assume the model constant is dimensionless. Similar-shape scaling does not apply to arbitrary changes in geometry.',
 [('The casting modulus increases by 50%, with equal C and n=2. Find the time ratio.','2.25, because 1.5²=2.25.'),('A solid cube has side 60 mm and all six faces cool. Find V/A.','10 mm. V/A=a³/(6a²)=a/6.')]),
chapter('machining-tool-life', 'Machining: cutting speed and tool life',
 'Cutting speed is the relative surface speed at the cutting zone, not the spindle rotational speed. In turning, connect the two through workpiece diameter and circumference. The basic Taylor tool-life relation is an empirical model for a specified tool-work combination and cutting conditions. Increasing cutting speed typically reduces tool life for a positive Taylor exponent. Compare two conditions using the ratio form so the constant cancels. Do not mix seconds and minutes or change the speed unit while keeping the same fitted constant. Feed and depth of cut influence machining performance and may require an extended model. Tool-life optimization also includes production time, tool-change time, cost and constraints; a maximum life by itself is not necessarily the economic operating point.',
 'Turning: V=πDN/1000 for D in mm, N in rpm, V in m/min. Taylor: VT^n=C. Ratio: T2/T1=(V1/V2)^(1/n).',
 'A tool lasts 80 min at 100 m/min. Find life at 200 m/min when Taylor exponent n=0.25 and the same model applies.',
 'T2=80×(100/200)^(1/0.25)=80×(0.5)^4=5 min. Doubling speed produces a large life reduction in this model. Verify by checking that VT^0.25 is the same at both conditions.',
 'The exponent for the life ratio is 1/n. Spindle rpm and cutting speed are not interchangeable.',
 [('Find cutting speed for D=100 mm and N=500 rpm.','157.08 m/min, from π×100×500/1000.'),('If speed is halved and n=0.5, how does modeled tool life change?','It becomes four times as long: (2)^(1/0.5)=4.')]),
chapter('limits-fits', 'Metrology: limits, fits and tolerance stacks',
 'A size tolerance is the difference between the permitted maximum and minimum size. A fit describes the possible relation between mating sizes. Calculate extreme clearance by pairing the largest hole with the smallest shaft and the smallest hole with the largest shaft. If every possible difference is positive, the pair has a clearance fit; if all are negative it has an interference fit; a range that crosses zero is a transition fit. A worst-case stack sums tolerance magnitudes when independently permitted extremes can align. Statistical combinations need assumptions about distributions, independence and process centering; a root-sum-square expression is not a universal replacement for the worst case. Use explicit limits before making a fit claim. A nominal size or CAD dimension alone does not establish tolerance or interchangeability.',
 'Cmax=Hmax−Smin; Cmin=Hmin−Smax. Size tolerance = upper limit − lower limit. Worst-case bilateral stack magnitude = Σti.',
 'Hole limits are 20.000–20.020 mm and shaft limits are 19.980–19.990 mm. Find the clearance range and fit type.',
 'Minimum clearance is 20.000−19.990=0.010 mm. Maximum is 20.020−19.980=0.040 mm. Both are positive, so the stated limits give a clearance fit. Hole tolerance is 0.020 mm; shaft tolerance is 0.010 mm.',
 'Use the opposing extremes for clearance. A zero clearance at one extreme needs precise classification rather than assuming a positive running clearance.',
 [('Hole: 10.000–10.010 mm; shaft: 10.020–10.030 mm. Find interference range.','0.010–0.030 mm. Minimum is Smin−Hmax; maximum is Smax−Hmin.'),('Two independent dimensions have bilateral limits ±0.1 and ±0.2 mm. Find worst-case stack tolerance magnitude.','±0.3 mm; add the magnitudes for the worst case.')]),
chapter('inventory-eoq', 'Industrial engineering: economic order quantity',
 'The basic economic order quantity model balances ordering cost against inventory holding cost. Larger lots reduce order frequency but raise average cycle inventory. The classic expression assumes constant known demand, instantaneous replenishment, no shortages, fixed order cost and no quantity discounts. Holding cost and demand must use the same time basis. Under these assumptions, average cycle stock is Q/2 and the number of orders per period is D/Q. At the economic optimum the ordering and cycle-stock holding costs are equal. Reorder point is a separate question about when to order: with deterministic demand and lead time it equals demand rate multiplied by lead time. Safety stock is unnecessary in this ideal deterministic model, but uncertain demand or lead time requires an additional service-level model.',
 'EOQ Q*=√(2DS/H). Relevant annual cost = DS/Q + HQ/2. Deterministic reorder point = demand rate × lead time.',
 'Annual demand D=10000 units, order cost S=200 rupees/order and annual holding cost H=4 rupees/unit. Find EOQ and the two relevant costs.',
 'Q*=√(2×10000×200/4)=1000 units. There are 10 orders/year. Annual ordering cost is 10000×200/1000=2000 rupees; holding cost is 4×1000/2=2000 rupees. Relevant total is 4000 rupees/year, excluding purchase cost.',
 'Do not combine monthly demand with annual holding cost. EOQ does not itself specify a reorder point or include safety stock.',
 [('If demand quadruples with S and H fixed, how does EOQ change?','It doubles because EOQ is proportional to √D.'),('Daily demand is 50 units and deterministic lead time is 6 days. Find the reorder point.','300 units, assuming the model has no safety stock.')])])
]
